- PR 4: `HAL_SPI_Init` / `HAL_I2C_Init` create the bus mutex once; lock
  helpers fail closed; I2C fallback pins aligned to `pdl_pins.h`.
- PR 5 (5a / 5b-1 / 5b-2 / 5c): kernel `event_dispatch_task` drains
  the event queue; `event_bus_subscribe` / `event_bus_dispatch` fan out
  to subscriber queues; `svc_time` is the first subscriber (RTC + NTP +
  POSIX TZ + 1 Hz tick).
- PR 6: live clock face — `ui_producer` 250 ms poll + render-on-change;
  `DM_MAX_TEXT_LEN` 8 to 16 with guaranteed NUL termination.
- PR 8: scalable time face — `HAL_GFX_BlitScaled`,
  `FONT_FORMAT_RGB565`, `DM_CMD_DRAW_TEXT_SCALED`, `FONT_UI_SANS`
  placeholder (TTF pending).
- PR 12: `svc_config` (NVS-backed installation config).
- PR 13: `svc_prayer_engine` (pure C++ math, 6 methods, 4 high-lat rules).
- PR 15: `svc_prayer_engine_service` (config + time + engine; publishes
  `EVENT_PRAYER_TIMES_UPDATED`).
- PR 17: midnight / DST rollover — `EVENT_TIME_DATE_CHANGED`, deferral
  until 00:01, noon-offset cache key.
- PR 18: Jafaria reachable; Isha and Midnight computed as offsets from
  wrapped sunset / maghrib so intervals stay correct at ~60 deg N.
- PR 19: iterated sun-position sampling per event (equinox accuracy
  from +/-125 s to +/-30 s vs AlAdhan).
- PR 4.1: `svc_http` — JSON REST APIs; `espressif/cjson` managed
  component via `idf_component.yml` (IDF 6 removed built-in `json`).
- PR 4.2: AP fallback (`ChronoLink-Setup`, `192.168.4.1`) plus AP setup
  screen in `ui_producer`.
- PR 4.3: web UI portal — `GET /` serves `index_html.c` (9133 bytes,
  generated by `scripts/embed_html.py`); `GET /api/prayers`,
  `POST /api/reboot`.
- PR 4.5: `POST /api/factory-reset` -> NVS `boot_req/flag` ->
  `boot_manager_init()` runs `do_factory_reset()` on next boot.
- PR 4.6: `CONFIG_BOOTLOADER_APP_ROLLBACK_ENABLE=y` plus
  `esp_ota_mark_app_valid_cancel_rollback()` after `core_os_start()`
  returns.
- PR 4.7: OTA over HTTP — `POST /api/ota` streams `.bin` to inactive
  slot; `X-OTA-Token` shared secret; `#error` gate if rollback off;
  `esp_ota_abort()` on any failure.

## Anti-patterns to reject immediately

- Adding `-DCONFIG_X=1` to `platformio.ini` build_flags
- Adding a new `Kconfig` entry that duplicates an existing pin
- Putting drawing primitives (Bresenham, clipping) in the HAL
- Kernel calling services
- Services calling HAL when a HIL adapter exists
- Referencing APIs that do not exist (check `docs/hal.md`, `docs/hil.md`)

## Git workflow (enforced)

Never commit directly to main. This applies to every change:
new features, fixes, CI config, docs, typo corrections, one-line
edits. There are no exceptions.

Every change goes through:

  1. `git checkout -b <type>/<short-name>`
     type is one of: feat, fix, chore, docs, ci, refactor, test
  2. edit + build + verify on hardware where applicable
  3. `git add -A`
     `git commit -m "<type>(<scope>): <subject>"`
  4. `git push -u origin <branch>`
  5. Open PR on GitHub (title + description with summary,
     scope, and verification section)
  6. Wait for CI. Do not merge with a red CI.
  7. Merge (squash and merge for multi-commit branches; regular
     merge commit is fine for single-commit branches)
  8. `git checkout main`
     `git pull origin main`
  9. `git push mirror main`
     `git fetch --prune`
 10. `git branch -d <branch>`
 11. Delete the remote branch on GitHub

If `git branch -d` refuses with "not fully merged", that is the
safety net doing its job — the commit is only on that branch.
Push it, open the PR, merge, then delete. Never use -D unless
the work is deliberately discarded.

Why this rule exists: on 2026-10-14 a CI config change was
committed directly to main in the interest of speed. It worked,
but it skipped CI-before-merge, broke the PR audit trail, and
set a precedent. Do not repeat.